{{!
	Shared closing tag for both pinnable container variants.

	Data dictionary supplied by NotionComponentPinnableContainer::getTemplateData(). This partial
	deliberately reads NEITHER key -- it is entirely context-free, which is exactly what lets one
	shared file serve every call site unchanged:
	string id container id stem, e.g. `notion-toc`. Consumed only by the Open partials, which
		append the `-pinned-container` / `-unpinned-container` suffixes. Those suffixes are a
		JavaScript contract resolved with document.getElementById, so they are never altered.
	bool is-pinned whether the pinnable element currently sits in the pinned placement. Callers
		guard the container's CONTENT with it; the container itself is never guarded.

	Closes the <div> opened by EITHER PinnableContainer/Pinned/Open.mustache OR
	PinnableContainer/Unpinned/Open.mustache. One shared closing partial serves both variants
	rather than duplicating a closing tag in each, which is why this file sits at the folder root
	while Pinned/ and Unpinned/ hold only their respective Open.mustache.

	Both containers are emitted on every render, regardless of the current is-pinned value.
	resources/skins.notion.js/pinnableElement.js relocates the pinnable region between the two
	containers at runtime with no page reload, so both move targets must already be present in
	the DOM. Whichever placement is not currently active is therefore legitimately empty, and the
	markup stays correct and fully styled when JavaScript is unavailable.

	Never emit this partial alone. Every caller that invokes an Open variant must invoke this
	partial exactly once, because LightnCandy will happily emit unbalanced HTML without raising
	anything -- a missing or surplus Close corrupts the page silently instead of failing loudly.
}}
